Intuition

For every element, we want to know the largest contiguous subarray in which that element can be the minimum.

For an element arr[i], if there is a smaller element on its left, then we cannot extend the subarray past that smaller element. The same applies to the right.

Therefore, using a monotonic stack, we find:

* leftMinIndex[i]: nearest element smaller than arr[i] on the left.
* rightMinIndex[i]: nearest element smaller than arr[i] on the right.

Then the largest subarray in which arr[i] can remain the minimum has length:

rightMinIndex[i] - leftMinIndex[i] - 1

Now consider the elements in descending order of value.

The largest value should be considered first because it gives the best possible answer for the smallest window sizes. For each element, we know the maximum window size for which it can be the minimum.

So, while processing elements from largest to smallest, we fill the still-unanswered window sizes that this element can support.

The key observation is:

If an element can be the minimum of a subarray of length L, then it can also be the minimum of some smaller subarray of every length up to L.

This lets us assign the current value to consecutive unanswered window sizes, while processing candidates from largest to smallest.

Approach

1. Use a monotonic increasing stack to find the nearest smaller element on the left for every index.
2. Use another monotonic increasing stack from right to left to find the nearest smaller element on the right.
3. For every element, calculate its maximum possible subarray length:

length = rightMinIndex[i] - leftMinIndex[i] - 1

4. Insert every element into a max heap.
    * Higher value has higher priority.
    * For equal values, use the maximum possible subarray length as a tie breaker.
5. Process the heap from largest value to smallest value.
6. For the current element, fill all still-unanswered window sizes that:
    * are not larger than its maximum valid length, and
    * are not larger than its position in the descending order.
7. Return the filled result array.

For example, for:

[10, 20, 30, 50, 10, 70, 30]

the largest value 70 can be the minimum only for a window of size 1, so it gives:

window size 1 -> 70

A value such as 30 can support a larger range, so once the better candidates for the smaller window sizes have been handled, 30 can fill the next available sizes that it supports.

Solution

import java.util.*;
class Solution {
    public static class Pair {
        int index;
        int value;
        Pair(int index, int value) {
            this.index = index;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }
    public static int[] maxOfMin(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] leftMinIndex = new int[n];
        int[] rightMinIndex = new int[n];
        // Find nearest smaller element on the left.
        Stack<Integer> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(0);
        leftMinIndex[0] = -1;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] >= arr[i]) {
                stack.pop();
            }
            leftMinIndex[i] = stack.isEmpty() ? -1 : stack.peek();
            stack.push(i);
        }
        // Find nearest smaller element on the right.
        stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push(n - 1);
        rightMinIndex[n - 1] = n;
        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            while (!stack.isEmpty() && arr[stack.peek()] >= arr[i]) {
                stack.pop();
            }
            rightMinIndex[i] = stack.isEmpty() ? n : stack.peek();
            stack.push(i);
        }
        // Process elements from largest value to smallest value.
        PriorityQueue<Pair> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>((a, b) -> {
            if (a.value != b.value) {
                return Integer.compare(b.value, a.value);
            }
            return Integer.compare(
                getMaxSubArrayLength(a.index, rightMinIndex, leftMinIndex),
                getMaxSubArrayLength(b.index, rightMinIndex, leftMinIndex)
            );
        });
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            maxHeap.add(new Pair(i, arr[i]));
        }
        int[] result = new int[n];
        int currentSolIndex = 0;
        while (!maxHeap.isEmpty()) {
            Pair pair = maxHeap.poll();
            int maxLength = getMaxSubArrayLength(
                pair.index,
                rightMinIndex,
                leftMinIndex
            );
            // Number of elements already processed, including current one.
            int sortedOrder = n - maxHeap.size();
            while (currentSolIndex + 1 <= maxLength
                    && currentSolIndex + 1 <= sortedOrder) {
                result[currentSolIndex] = pair.value;
                currentSolIndex++;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
    private static int getMaxSubArrayLength(
            int index,
            int[] rightMinIndex,
            int[] leftMinIndex) {
        return rightMinIndex[index] - leftMinIndex[index] - 1;
    }
}

Complexity

Time Complexity:
O(n log n)

The nearest smaller element calculations using monotonic stacks take O(n) because every index is pushed and popped at most once.

Each of the n elements is inserted into the PriorityQueue and removed once, costing O(log n) per operation.

Therefore, the total complexity is:

O(n) + O(n) + O(n log n) = O(n log n)

The inner while loop does not make the solution O(n²) because currentSolIndex only moves forward from 0 to n - 1 once across the entire execution.

Space Complexity:
O(n)

We use:

* leftMinIndex → O(n)
* rightMinIndex → O(n)
* monotonic stack → O(n)
* priority queue → O(n)
* result array → O(n)

Therefore, the overall auxiliary space complexity is O(n).54:["$","$L
